From 8028714acd572809f0f28157711e20b9554da32c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 23:39:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 44/53] util/mesa_cache_db: gate on flock, like the Fossilize
 database beside it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 7 item 1 (Mesa shader disk cache on Horizon)
Why: mesa_cache_db.c calls flock(), guarded only by
 `#if DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0`. A C library can perfectly well declare
 flock in <sys/file.h> and not implement it, and then the file compiles
 and the link fails — which is worse than a compile error, because it
 fails in whatever binary happens to pull the object in rather than
 where the problem is.

 The file beside it already handles exactly this: fossilize_db.h defines
 FOZ_DB_UTIL only `#ifdef HAVE_FLOCK`, and fossilize_db.c compiles to
 stubs without it. mesa_cache_db.h even carries the complete set of
 inline stubs already — they were simply reachable only on Windows. This
 makes the condition match the sibling's, so the same absence produces
 the same graceful result.
Evidence: measured on devkitA64 gcc 15.2.0 (newlib). Mesa's own configure
 probe, which is a link test, answers no, and the file therefore compiles
 and then does not link. Upstream's mesa_cache_db.c, compiled verbatim
 with this build's flags:

   $ aarch64-none-elf-gcc @args -c mesa_cache_db.c -o mcdb.o     # succeeds
   $ aarch64-none-elf-nm -u mcdb.o | grep -E 'flock|ftruncate|fileno'
                    U fileno
                    U flock
                    U ftruncate

 and flock is genuinely not there:

   $ cat fl.c
   #include <sys/file.h>
   int main(void){ return flock(0, LOCK_EX); }
   $ aarch64-none-elf-gcc -specs=switch.specs fl.c -lnx
   ld: fl.c:(.text+0x10): undefined reference to `flock'
   collect2: error: ld returned 1 exit status

 (posix_fallocate and memfd_create are absent the same way, by link
 probe; neither is reached from this file.)

 mesa_cache_db_multipart.c needs no change: it calls only the
 mesa_cache_db_* entry points, which the header's existing #else branch
 already provides, and the one system call it makes itself is mkdir.
Upstream: yes — the condition is a libc trait, written the way the file
 next to it writes it, and no build that defined HAVE_FLOCK before takes
 a different branch after.
---
 src/util/mesa_cache_db.c | 4 ++--
 src/util/mesa_cache_db.h | 4 ++--
 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/mesa_cache_db.c b/src/util/mesa_cache_db.c
index 0daac81de12..ed10c27a5f5 100644
--- a/src/util/mesa_cache_db.c
+++ b/src/util/mesa_cache_db.c
@@ -9,7 +9,7 @@
 
 #include "detect_os.h"
 
-#if DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0
+#if DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0 && defined(HAVE_FLOCK)
 
 #include <fcntl.h>
 #include <stddef.h>
@@ -1076,4 +1076,4 @@ fail:
    return 0;
 }
 
-#endif /* DETECT_OS_WINDOWS */
+#endif /* DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0 && HAVE_FLOCK */
diff --git a/src/util/mesa_cache_db.h b/src/util/mesa_cache_db.h
index f6097d81271..6fd4f7c735a 100644
--- a/src/util/mesa_cache_db.h
+++ b/src/util/mesa_cache_db.h
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@ struct mesa_cache_db {
    bool alive;
 };
 
-#if DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0
+#if DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0 && defined(HAVE_FLOCK)
 bool
 mesa_cache_db_open(struct mesa_cache_db *db, const char *cache_path);
 
@@ -139,7 +139,7 @@ mesa_cache_db_eviction_score(struct mesa_cache_db *db)
 {
    return 0;
 }
-#endif /* DETECT_OS_WINDOWS */
+#endif /* DETECT_OS_WINDOWS == 0 && HAVE_FLOCK */
 
 #ifdef __cplusplus
 }
-- 
